<details><summary>Click to reveal the finite-workload theorem</summary>

For any finite set $S$, summing the pointwise bounds gives
$$
\left(\sum_{n\in S}b(n)\right)k_BT\ln2\le\sum_{n\in S}\mathcal L(e_n;k_B,T).
$$
The result needs no regularity or consecutiveness assumption on $S$.

</details>

## Unbounded loss, unbounded floor

At fixed positive temperature, an unbounded bit-loss guarantee cannot have a bounded Landauer floor. The witness finder converts any requested energy threshold into a sufficient input size for a linear family.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

The final chart isolates temperature as a physical scaling parameter.

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the unboundedness argument</summary>

Set $L=k_BT\ln2>0$. Given an energy threshold $E$, choose $n$ with $E/L<b(n)$. Then $E<b(n)L\le\mathcal L(e_n;k_B,T)$.

</details>

For broader background on entropy and nonuniform inputs, see [Shannon entropy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Entropy_(information_theory)); for the architectural alternative, see [reversible computing](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reversible_computing).
